  <static seed="127" source="Library/METU-NCC/Applied_Math/least-squares/parabola.pg">
    <statement><p>By using the method of least squares, find the best parabola through the 
points:</p>
<p><m>(2, -3)</m>,   
<m>(1, 1)</m>,   
<m>(0, -3)</m>,   
<m>(-1, 0)</m></p>
<p><alert> Step 1.</alert>  The general equation of a parabola is 
 <m>c_0 + c_1x + c_2x^2 = y</m>.  Plugging the data points into this formula 
gives a matrix equation <m>\mathrm{A}\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{y}</m>.</p>
<p><fillin cols="3" rows="4"/> <m>\left[\begin{matrix}c_0\\c_1\\c_2\end{matrix}\right]</m>
   <m>=</m>  <fillin rows="4"/></p>
<p><alert> Step 2.</alert> The matrix equation 
<m>\mathrm{A}\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{y}</m> has 
no solution, so instead we use the <alert>normal equation</alert>
<m>\mathrm{A^T} \mathrm{A}\, \hat{\mathbf{c}} = \mathrm{A^T} \mathbf{y}</m></p>
<p><m>\mathrm{A^T} \mathrm{A} =</m> <fillin cols="3" rows="3"/></p>
<p><m>\mathrm{A^T} \mathbf{y} =</m> <fillin rows="3"/></p>
<p><alert> Step 3.</alert> Solving the normal equation gives the answer</p>
<p><m>\hat{\mathbf{c}} =</m> <fillin rows="3"/></p>
<p>which corresponds to the formula</p>
<p><m>y =</m> <fillin characters="50" name="AnSwEr0006"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-0.75+\left(-0.25\right)x+\left(-0.25\right)x^{2}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
